# How to Use This Document

## What this topic covers

Topic 6 is syllabus **Head VII — Gender and Governance**. The syllabus names six lines: **defining governance**; the **suffragist movement**; gender issues in women as **voters**, as **candidates** and as **representatives**; the **impact** of women representatives on politics; and **case studies** of Sri Lanka, Pakistan, India, Bangladesh and Nepal. This document teaches all of them, and gives the most space to the line the examiner loves most: **political quotas for women in Pakistan**.

In the descriptive paper Topic 6 has had ==8.5 visits==: eight full questions and one short note. **Quotas** dominate — 2016, 2021, 2024 and 2025 all asked about them — and **women's political participation** follows (2017, 2018, 2024). Women as **representatives** (2022) and women in **leadership** (2020) complete the record. The topic also supplies **14 of the 140 MCQs**, mostly firsts and numbers: the **two** women of the first Constituent Assembly, **60** reserved seats, **33%** under devolution, **Benazir Bhutto**, **Fahmida Mirza**, **New Zealand** ==1893==.

::: simple
**Governance** is how a country is run — who makes decisions, how, and for whom. **Gender and governance** asks a simple question: are women **present** where decisions are made, and are their **interests** served by those decisions? In Pakistan, women can vote, stand and sit in parliament, but at every step — voting, contesting, representing, leading — they meet barriers men do not.
:::

## How this document is built

1. **Part Zero** gives you the words of the topic and the "ladder" of political participation.
2. **Part One** has ten chapters: governance and representation (1); the suffrage movement (2); women as voters (3), as candidates (4) and as representatives (5); gender quotas — types and arguments (6); the history of Pakistan's quota (7); the impact of women representatives (8); women in leadership (9); and the South Asian case studies (10).
3. **Part Two** sets out today's debates.
4. **Part Three** lists every past question, what the record shows, and five likely questions.
5. **Part Four** is a bank of about 120 one-liners, followed by every past MCQ on the topic.
6. **Part Five** is the revision sheet.

# Part Zero — The ABC of Topic 6

## 0.1  The words of this topic

::: table | The key terms of Topic 6 (★ = you must be able to define it in one sentence).
@widths 3.6,10.4
Term | Simple meaning, with an example
Governance ★ | The way power is used to manage a country's affairs — by the state, and also by markets and civil society.
Good governance ★ | Governance that is **participatory, accountable, transparent, responsive, effective, equitable** and follows the **rule of law**.
Gender-responsive governance | Governance in which women take part in decisions and policies serve women's and men's needs fairly.
Suffrage ★ | The **right to vote** (MCQ 2019). "Women's suffrage" = women's right to vote.
Suffragist | A campaigner for votes for women, especially one using peaceful, lawful methods.
Suffragette | A militant British campaigner for women's votes (the WSPU, from ==1903==).
Universal adult franchise | Every adult citizen may vote, regardless of sex, property or education.
Political participation ★ | Taking part in politics — voting, campaigning, joining parties, standing for office, protesting.
Representation ★ | Acting for others in decision-making. **Descriptive**: the representative resembles the people; **substantive**: she acts for their interests.
Politics of presence | Anne Phillips's argument (==1995==) that **who** represents matters, not only **what ideas** are represented.
Critical mass ★ | The idea that women must reach a certain share — often **30%** — before they change an institution.
Token | A member of a small minority (under about 15%) who is highly visible and pressured to conform (Kanter ==1977==).
Gender quota ★ | A rule requiring a minimum share or number of women in a body or on candidate lists.
Reserved seats ★ | Seats only women can fill. Pakistan: ==60== of 336 in the National Assembly.
Legislated candidate quota | A law requiring parties to nominate a share of women candidates — e.g. Pakistan's **5%** of general-seat tickets (==2017==).
Voluntary party quota | A party's own rule to field women, e.g. many European parties.
Proportional representation (PR) | Seats shared by parties according to their votes, often from lists. Pakistan's reserved seats are filled this way.
First-past-the-post (FPTP) | The candidate with the most votes in a constituency wins. Pakistan's general seats use it.
Gender gap in voting ★ | The difference between men's and women's registration or turnout. Pakistan 2024: ==9.94 million== fewer women registered.
Glass ceiling ★ | An invisible barrier that stops women reaching top posts.
Glass cliff ★ | Women promoted to leadership in a **crisis**, when the risk of failure is high (MCQ 2026; Ryan and Haslam ==2005==).
Queen bee | A woman leader who distances herself from other women instead of helping them.
Women's Parliamentary Caucus | A cross-party forum of women legislators in Pakistan, formed in ==2008==.
:::

## 0.2  The ladder of women's political participation

Political participation is like a ladder. At each rung, fewer women remain.

::: fig pyramid | The ladder of women's political participation in Pakistan — fewer women at each higher rung.
Leaders: prime minister, chief minister, ministers — a handful (zero women ministers in 2025)
Representatives: about one-fifth of the National Assembly, mostly on reserved seats
Candidates: 306 women on general NA seats in 2024 — a small share of all candidates; 11 won
Voters: 59.3 million women registered (46.1%); turnout 43% against 52% for men
Citizens: women are about half of Pakistan's 241 million people
:::

::: eye | How the examiner uses this topic
Most Topic 6 questions ask you to **judge** something: "pros and cons of gender quota… impact in Pakistan" (2016); "Is Pakistan's gender quota… showing results?" (2021); "Is political participation a human right?… impact of the political quota system" (2024); "historical context and evolution of women's quota in Pakistan" (2025). Learn for every answer: (1) the **theory** (representation, critical mass, quota types); (2) Pakistan's **history** — 1956, 1962, 1973, 1985, the lapse, 2002, 2017; (3) the **current numbers** from the ==2024== election; (4) the **barriers**; and (5) a **balanced judgement** with reforms.
:::
